<body><main><small>THE GLASS SAUSAGE FACTORY / GEOMETRY LABORATORY / SANDBOXED</small><h1>Whirligig · R/Q</h1><p>The R and Q curve followers slide simultaneously around opposite east/west hemispheres of the same sphere. Their moving crossbar carries a perpendicular gimbal elbow; that elbow itself reaches the torus wall and draws the composite <i>Curvy Lisa</i> trace.</p>
<div class="bar"><button id="play">Pause</button><button id="reset">Restart</button><button id="svg">Export Curvy Lisa SVG</button><select id="preset" aria-label="Equation pair"><option value="base">R/Q harmonics (archive example)</option><option value="first">First pair harmonics</option><option value="flat">Flat R, oscillating Q</option></select><button id="trail">Hide guide lines</button></div>
<div class="row"><div class="plate"><span>TORUS + EQUAL-DIAMETER SPHERE · DRAG TO ORBIT</span><canvas id="mechanism" aria-label="3D projected coupled Whirligig apparatus"></canvas></div><div class="plate"><span>CURVY LISA · ELBOW TIP TRACE</span><canvas id="output" aria-label="Live mechanically coupled output"></canvas></div></div>
<div class="controls">
<label>Equation R amplitude <output id="vr"></output><input data-key="ra" type="range" min="0" max="1" step=".01" value=".55"></label>
<label>Equation Q amplitude <output id="vq"></output><input data-key="qa" type="range" min="0" max="1" step=".01" value=".60"></label>
<label>Q phase <output id="vp"></output><input data-key="ph" type="range" min="-3.14" max="3.14" step=".02" value="0"></label>
<label>Elbow bearing turns <output id="vg1"></output><input data-key="g1" type="range" min="1" max="6" step="1" value="1"></label>
<label>Pen articulation modulation <output id="vg2"></output><input data-key="g2" type="range" min="0" max="1.5" step=".02" value=".32"></label>
<label>Elbow revolutions <output id="vpt"></output><input data-key="pt" type="range" min="0" max="8" step="1" value="4"></label>
<label>Major radius R <output id="vmajor"></output><input data-key="major" type="range" min="1.25" max="2.4" step=".05" value="1.65"></label>
<label>Tube & sphere radius a=b <output id="va"></output><input data-key="minor" type="range" min=".3" max=".8" step=".05" value=".55"></label>
<label>Time fraction <output id="vt"></output><input id="time" type="range" min="0" max="1" step=".002" value="0"></label>
</div><div id="stats" class="stats" role="status">Computing geometry…</div>
<p class="foot">This is a browser reconstruction of the historical <b>b=a</b> limiting setup. Its selected contact obeys the analytic no-slip condition at one nominated point; because equal radii create a nonisolated contact set, this does <b>not</b> certify no-slip across every point of contact. There is no separate pen or projector. The only tracing member is the gimbal elbow, constrained perpendicular to the moving crossbar. Two independently shaped curves are traversed by one shared clock; signed curvature differences change elbow orientation. The mechanism is a geometric reconstruction, not a proven physical bearing model. No isomorphism or physical law is assumed. Research reference: <a href="https://github.com/Satobloc/HsH/tree/main/tools/whirligig_rq">Whirligig R/Q Python solver</a>.</p></main>
